#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120567} :activity/id #uuid "dc0787d2-66e7-5426-a321-949ba2e7c51b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050196 :topic/id #uuid "35ae4fb0-3dd4-562e-8f32-3a2e1f7470e1" :topic/math-academy-id 1796} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120997 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function $$\\mathbf f (t)= \\big\\langle2 \\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: t \\big\\rangle.$$ At the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},$ the function, unit tangent, and the principal normal are given by\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨6,\\,-6\\sqrt{3},\\,5\\pi ⟩ \\\\ T(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-\\sqrt{15},\\,-\\sqrt{5},\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince the unit tangent $\\mathbf T$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N$ are not collinear, they define a *unique* plane, called the **osculating plane.** In general, the osculating plane to a curve at a point $P$ is the plane of \"greatest contact\" with the curve at $P.$\n\n![](images/0a/0af5fd8c4fce63bbc15fe35d40e20602fbd1420bcd23f875368ddcb76755cb79.png)\n\nSince the osculating plane contains the unit tangent vector and the principal normal vector, we can write the equation of the osculating plane in parametric form as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+s\\,T(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+u\\,N(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨6,\\,-6\\sqrt{3},\\,5\\pi ⟩+\\frac{s}{5}⟨-\\sqrt{15},\\,-\\sqrt{5},\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩+\\frac{u}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}$$ where $s, u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nIn general, for a curve $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ the osculating plane at the point $t=t_0$ has the parametric equation $$\\mathbf R(s,u) = \\mathbf r \\left( t_0 \\right) + s \\, \\mathbf T \\left( t_0 \\right) + u \\, \\mathbf N \\left( t_0 \\right), \\qquad s, u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),$$ where $\\mathbf{T}(t_0)$ and $\\mathbf{N}(t_0)$ are the unit tangent and the principal normal to the curve at $t=t_0,$ respectively.\n\n**Note:** A straight line does not have a principal normal, and consequently it does not have an osculating plane either." :tutorial/id #uuid "47992101-e151-57a2-9a0f-aa58c4db168f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2370 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1ed71b2f-4ce7-54f0-b2bc-730ad0cfda29" :step/math-academy-id 10274 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120568}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119556 :question/id #uuid "b1d808a6-49e1-54ae-88c7-36c42f93e179" :question/math-academy-id "e-3060"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393786}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"} :step/id #uuid "44c831c7-9b75-5efc-8fa9-ddb3c7b148d3" :step/math-academy-id 10275 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120998 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a curve $C$ is parametrized by the vector function $\\mathbf f(t).$ Given a point $P$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf p,$ we can find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane at $P$ using the following three steps.\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we calculate the unit tangent $\\mathbf T(t_0)$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N(t_0).$\n- **Step 2:** Next, we calculate the cross product $\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf T(t_0) \\times \\mathbf N(t_0).$ Recall that $\\mathbf{n}$ will be perpendicular to the plane containing both the tangent $\\mathbf{T}(t_0)$ and normal $\\mathbf{N}(t_0).$\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we write down the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane: $$\\big( \\langle x, y, z \\rangle - \\mathbf{p} \\big) \\cdot \\mathbf n = 0$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c56b88c4-34db-5020-8461-5d9295e3dc51" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2371 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"} :step/id #uuid "04b3287e-7857-5988-8e8d-faaa11fc45a6" :step/math-academy-id 10278 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119557 :question/id #uuid "0ecbb0ee-5559-55c3-9aba-0056288a6c67" :question/math-academy-id "e-3061"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377728}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"} :step/id #uuid "36ec013b-6152-55b4-abd3-ca3407f4ed05" :step/math-academy-id 10279 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120571}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119558 :question/id #uuid "7b4c22d8-65f0-5a5e-9eb5-754f778a7bf7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3062"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049322}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377228}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "6b8cab73-ce4b-5307-9379-d3f0465cede8" :step/math-academy-id 10282}] :activity/title "The Osculating Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120014}]}
